From 92cf95720aff1c0c80834539a53da2e2d1b11b62 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 00:43:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Record the fODF glyph amplitude measurement in the handoff

Packages the results of the Figure 7 glyph-size question for the next
agent, and reverts every code change proposed after the user asked about
those figures. The manuscript file is untouched relative to master.

Adds deconv_comparison/measure_glyph_spread.m: 32 independent noise
realisations per SNR through the real MRtrix arms, scoring the maximum
clamped glyph radius after the p_00 = 1 normalisation Figure 7 applies.
One dwi2fod call per SNR, about four minutes.

The result splits by arm, and one half of it is new:

- SSST-CSD's glyph size is the noise draw. Median radius moves 1.52x
  across the whole SNR sweep while the spread within a single SNR
  reaches 2.32x. Figure 7 draws one arbitrary realisation per SNR, so
  which panel looks big is mostly which draw landed first in the array.
- MSMT-CSD's is real and large. Median radius moves 7.15x monotonically,
  0.286 at SNR 5 to 2.043 noise free -- three to four times the
  within-SNR spread. In edema its white-matter fODF amplitude is
  strongly SNR-dependent, which bears on any amplitude-derived measure
  from MSMT-CSD in edematous tissue and has not been followed up.

Handoff changes:

- section 2.2 records two wrong answers given to the user's question
  before this one, and why each was wrong: an autoscaling claim verified
  only under Octave when the user renders in MATLAB, and a trend
  statistic quoted at a figure that draws a single realisation. That
  second one was additionally computed from a retyped edema kernel
  rather than the manuscript's, which hid the MSMT effect entirely;
- section 6.6 is the measurement, with both tables and the caveat;
- section 8 opens with the user's standing request -- still show the
  effect of SNR on fODF appearance -- the per-arm constraint on any
  design, and an explicit instruction not to re-propose the GLYPH_SCALE
  and axis-pinning work, which was reverted for fixing the rendering
  without fixing what was being rendered;
- section 10 records that figure changes are direction-first.

